content={
'Agentic AI Automation Business — Project Index':'''AGENTIC AI AUTOMATION BUSINESS — PROJECT INDEX\n\nSTATUS\nSeparate business venture. This project is not part of Algorithm Academy. All canonical business assets live in this Google Drive folder under affan.fareed@gmail.com until a dedicated business Workspace account is justified.\n\nMISSION\nHelp small businesses turn agentic AI into measurable business results by installing, configuring, securing, and continuously improving Hermes-powered AI operators.\n\nCORE POSITIONING\nWe install a secure AI operator inside your business, connect it to the tools you already use, and train it to complete valuable work—not merely answer questions.\n\nWHAT WE SELL\n• Completed business outcomes\n• Workflow design and automation\n• Hermes installation and configuration\n• Integrations with approved business systems\n• Persistent company knowledge and reusable skills\n• Scheduled and event-triggered operations\n• Human approval gates\n• Monitoring, maintenance, and continuous capability growth\n\nINITIAL MARKET\nOwner-operated service businesses with costly lead leakage, slow follow-up, repetitive administration, inconsistent marketing, or information trapped in the owner’s head.\n\nINITIAL PRODUCT\nThe AI Front Office: a managed agentic system for lead handling, customer-request organization, approved response drafting, follow-up, owner briefings, review requests, and operational support.\n\nFOLDER GUIDE\n01 Positioning and Offers — promise, packages, pricing, boundaries\n02 Capability Catalog — marketable capability library\n03 Sales and Marketing — pitches, flier, landing page, outreach\n04 Discovery and Audit — qualification, workflow and ROI discovery\n05 Proposals and Onboarding — scope, access, launch checklists\n06 Delivery and Operations — implementation, support, reporting\n07 Security and Governance — permissions, approvals, data handling\n08 Finance and ROI — calculator, assumptions, pricing economics\n09 Case Studies — proof templates and future results\n10 Client Delivery — one controlled folder per customer\n''',
'Offer Architecture and Service Packages':'''OFFER ARCHITECTURE AND SERVICE PACKAGES\n\nCUSTOMER-FACING PROMISE\nA managed AI operator that performs approved work across your business, reports what it completed, and gains useful capabilities as your company grows.\n\n1. AI OPPORTUNITY AUDIT — $750\n• 60–90 minute owner interview\n• Workflow and bottleneck inventory\n• Repetitive-task and lead-leak analysis\n• Security and system-access assessment\n• Prioritized automation roadmap\n• Conservative ROI estimate\n• Fixed-price first implementation proposal\nCredit the audit fee toward implementation when the customer signs within 30 days.\n\n2. AGENTIC STARTER — $3,500 SETUP + $1,250/MONTH\n• Hermes installation and health verification\n• One business agent profile\n• One communication channel\n• One high-value automated workflow\n• One recurring owner briefing\n• Basic company knowledge base\n• Approval gates and activity reporting\n• Documentation and owner training\n• Monthly maintenance and limited optimization\nClient pays model, hosting, and third-party software costs directly. Suggested initial term: three managed-service months.\n\n3. MANAGED AI OPERATOR — $6,500 SETUP + $2,250/MONTH\n• Multiple connected workflows\n• Workspace/email/calendar/document integration where approved\n• Custom company skills and procedures\n• Sales, service, or operations support\n• Scheduled reports and exception alerts\n• Monitoring and priority maintenance\n• One meaningful capability improvement per month\n• Quarterly automation roadmap\n\n4. AGENTIC GROWTH PARTNER — FROM $12,000 SETUP + $4,500/MONTH\n• Multiple agents or isolated profiles\n• Custom APIs, webhooks, and data integrations\n• Cross-functional growth and operations workflows\n• Advanced monitoring and support\n• Monthly strategy and implementation cycle\n• ROI reporting and continuous experimentation\nSell only after strong proof, processes, and testimonials exist.\n\nSCOPE RULES\nEvery package defines number of workflows, integrations, users, support response target, monthly change allowance, variable usage costs, approval requirements, and change-order triggers. Never promise unlimited automation or unlimited AI usage.\n''',
'Marketable Capability Catalog':'''MARKETABLE CAPABILITY CATALOG\n\nUse this as a pitch library. Select only the capabilities relevant to each prospect and connect them to revenue, time, risk, or customer experience.\n\nSALES AND LEADS\n• Capture inquiries from approved forms, email, and spreadsheets\n• Research and enrich prospects\n• Classify and prioritize leads\n• Draft personalized responses for approval\n• Alert staff about urgent or high-value opportunities\n• Prepare sales-call briefs\n• Generate first-draft proposals and estimates\n• Track unanswered leads and dormant opportunities\n• Produce pipeline and follow-up summaries\n\nCUSTOMER SERVICE\n• Monitor shared support channels\n• Categorize requests and retrieve approved answers\n• Draft consistent responses\n• Escalate sensitive, unusual, or high-risk cases\n• Create and update knowledge bases\n• Summarize long customer conversations\n• Identify recurring complaints and service opportunities\n• Prepare follow-up and review-request drafts\n\nMARKETING AND GROWTH\n• Research customer questions and competitor activity\n• Build content calendars\n• Draft newsletters, posts, blogs, and lead magnets\n• Repurpose long content into shorter formats\n• Maintain approved brand voice\n• Organize campaign assets\n• Analyze performance and recommend next topics\n• Prepare local and seasonal campaign ideas\n\nADMINISTRATION\n• Organize Drive or Microsoft 365 materials\n• Draft routine documents and reports\n• Turn meeting notes into actions\n• Maintain SOPs, templates, and internal knowledge\n• Track deadlines and incoming requests\n• Route information to the right person\n• Prepare daily owner briefings\n\nEMAIL AND CALENDAR\n• Identify priority messages\n• Summarize important threads\n• Draft replies for approval\n• Route leads, invoices, vendors, and support requests\n• Schedule or prepare meetings with approval\n• Generate agendas, research, notes, and follow-ups\n• Detect unanswered correspondence\n\nFINANCE SUPPORT — HUMAN CONTROL REQUIRED\n• Organize receipts and invoices\n• Extract information into review queues\n• Identify missing paperwork\n• Draft payment reminders\n• Compare approved vendor information\n• Prepare expense and cash-flow summaries\n• Flag anomalies for human review\nNever autonomously transfer money, change bank information, make tax decisions, or present accounting/legal advice.\n\nPEOPLE AND TRAINING\n• Draft job descriptions and interview guides\n• Summarize applications using approved criteria\n• Create onboarding and training checklists\n• Maintain employee knowledge resources\n• Track approved training and certification data\n• Convert expert knowledge into reusable procedures\n\nRESEARCH AND DECISION SUPPORT\n• Monitor competitors, vendors, and market developments\n• Summarize customer feedback\n• Compare products, pricing, and opportunities\n• Prepare decision briefs and SWOT analyses\n• Produce recurring executive reports\n\nTECHNICAL AND DIGITAL OPERATIONS\n• Monitor websites, forms, jobs, and integrations\n• Identify broken pages and failed workflows\n• Draft landing pages and documentation\n• Automate approved data transfers\n• Connect APIs, webhooks, databases, and MCP tools\n• Prepare incident summaries and maintenance reports\n\nHERMES-POWERED ADVANTAGES\n• Persistent memory and company-specific skills\n• Scheduled jobs and event-triggered webhooks\n• Browser, terminal, document, and research tools\n• Messaging access through familiar platforms\n• Multiple isolated profiles and specialist agents\n• Human approval gates and auditable reports\n• Provider flexibility and extensibility\n''',
'AI Front Office — Flagship Offer':'''AI FRONT OFFICE — FLAGSHIP OFFER\n\nHEADLINE\nStop losing leads and owner time to an overloaded front office.\n\nPROMISE\nWe install and manage an AI operator that helps your business respond faster, organize customer requests, prepare follow-ups, and show the owner what needs attention.\n\nPROBLEMS IT TARGETS\n• Leads wait too long for a response\n• Important email gets buried\n• Estimates and follow-ups are inconsistent\n• The owner manually checks several systems\n• Review requests are forgotten\n• Customer knowledge lives in one employee’s head\n• Marketing happens only when there is spare time\n\nSTARTER WORKFLOW\n1. Monitor an approved intake source.\n2. Classify lead, customer, vendor, billing, or low-priority message.\n3. Extract required details.\n4. Draft the next action or approved response.\n5. Ask for human approval where required.\n6. Update the approved tracker or system.\n7. Alert the responsible person.\n8. Include unresolved items in the owner briefing.\n\nDELIVERABLES\n• Working Hermes installation\n• Secure profile and credential design\n• One lead/customer workflow\n• Owner briefing\n• Company knowledge starter pack\n• Approval and escalation rules\n• Testing evidence and handoff guide\n• Managed monitoring and improvement\n\nSUCCESS METRICS\nMedian lead-response time; leads requiring follow-up; owner hours saved; number of approved drafts; unresolved customer requests; review requests sent; appointments or estimates generated; workflow error rate.\n\nNOT INCLUDED BY DEFAULT\nAutonomous financial transfers, legal or medical decisions, unapproved public posting, unrestricted access to every company system, custom enterprise software, or unlimited monthly changes.\n''',
'Sales Discovery Script':'''SALES DISCOVERY SCRIPT\n\nOPENING\n“We help small businesses install a managed AI operator that completes approved work across the tools they already use. I’d like to understand where time, leads, or customer requests currently fall through the cracks.”\n\nBUSINESS CONTEXT\n1. What does the business sell, and which service is most profitable?\n2. How do new leads usually arrive?\n3. What happens from first inquiry to paid customer?\n4. Which tasks still require the owner every day?\n5. Where do delays or mistakes occur most often?\n\nVALUE DISCOVERY\n6. How many hours per week does this process consume?\n7. Whose time is involved and what is that time worth?\n8. How many leads or customers are lost because of delay?\n9. What is an average new customer worth?\n10. What would improve if this process were reliable?\n\nSYSTEM DISCOVERY\n11. Which email, calendar, CRM, messaging, document, and accounting systems are used?\n12. Is there an existing SOP or is the process mostly informal?\n13. Which data is sensitive?\n14. Which actions must always require human approval?\n15. Who owns each system and can authorize access?\n\nQUALIFICATION\n16. Is the problem frequent and measurable?\n17. Is there one owner for implementation?\n18. Can we access test data or a safe pilot environment?\n19. Is the buyer prepared to change the process, not merely add software?\n20. What would make a 30-day pilot obviously successful?\n\nCLOSE\nRecommend a paid AI Opportunity Audit. Do not design the complete system for free during discovery.\n''',
'AI Opportunity Audit Questionnaire':'''AI OPPORTUNITY AUDIT QUESTIONNAIRE\n\nCLIENT DETAILS\nBusiness name; owner; industry; locations; employee count; approximate lead volume; core systems; decision maker; technical contact.\n\nWORKFLOW INVENTORY\nFor every candidate workflow record: trigger, inputs, steps, owner, systems, frequency, average minutes, wait time, common errors, exceptions, approval points, output, and downstream consequence.\n\nSCORING (1–5 EACH)\n• Frequency\n• Time consumed\n• Revenue impact\n• Error/risk reduction\n• Process consistency\n• Data accessibility\n• Ease of safe testing\n• Executive urgency\nSubtract points for high sensitivity, undefined ownership, unstable processes, or unclear success criteria.\n\nROI INPUTS\nOccurrences per month; minutes per occurrence; loaded hourly cost; monthly error/rework cost; leads lost; customer value; conservative recovery percentage; monthly software/model cost; setup cost; monthly management fee.\n\nSECURITY REVIEW\nData classes involved; credentials needed; least-privilege access available; audit logs; retention requirements; approved communication channels; prohibited actions; required human review; incident contact.\n\nAUDIT DELIVERABLE\n1. Current-state map\n2. Top automation candidates\n3. Recommended first workflow\n4. Risks and controls\n5. Conservative ROI range\n6. Implementation scope\n7. Timeline and customer responsibilities\n8. Fixed-price proposal\n''',
'Proposal and Scope Template':'''PROPOSAL AND SCOPE TEMPLATE\n\nCLIENT: [Name]\nPROJECT: [Outcome-focused project title]\nDATE: [Date]\nVALID THROUGH: [Date]\n\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n[Describe the current business problem, measurable consequence, and proposed result.]\n\nTARGET OUTCOME\n[Example: reduce median lead-response time and ensure every qualified inquiry receives an approved next action.]\n\nIN SCOPE\n• Systems and approved data sources\n• Exact workflow triggers and steps\n• Agent responsibilities\n• Human approval points\n• Integrations\n• Reports and alerts\n• Training and documentation\n• Testing and acceptance criteria\n\nOUT OF SCOPE\nList autonomous payments, legal/medical decisions, public publishing, unrelated custom development, unlimited changes, unsupported systems, and anything else excluded.\n\nSUCCESS METRICS\nDefine baseline, target, measurement source, review frequency, and who confirms acceptance.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION PHASES\nDiscovery confirmation; access and security setup; safe prototype; test-data validation; controlled pilot; staff training; production launch; managed optimization.\n\nCOMMERCIAL TERMS\nSetup fee; monthly managed-service fee; third-party and model costs; payment schedule; initial term; change orders; late payments; suspension conditions.\n\nCUSTOMER RESPONSIBILITIES\nProvide authorized access, accurate process information, test cases, decision makers, timely reviews, and lawful data use.\n\nIMPORTANT\nUse this operational template as input to a professionally drafted service agreement. It is not legal advice.\n''',
'Client Onboarding and Access Checklist':'''CLIENT ONBOARDING AND ACCESS CHECKLIST\n\nBUSINESS\n□ Signed scope and commercial terms\n□ Payment received\n□ Executive sponsor named\n□ Day-to-day workflow owner named\n□ Success metrics and baseline recorded\n□ Escalation contact recorded\n\nSYSTEMS\n□ Inventory approved systems\n□ Use client-owned accounts where possible\n□ Create dedicated automation identities\n□ Grant least privilege\n□ Separate credentials by client and purpose\n□ Store secrets outside documents and source control\n□ Verify revocation procedure\n□ Record API/model cost owner\n\nWORKFLOW\n□ Confirm trigger, steps, exceptions, and outputs\n□ Collect positive and negative test cases\n□ Define prohibited actions\n□ Define approval gates\n□ Define failure behavior\n□ Define duplicate prevention\n□ Define audit/report format\n\nLAUNCH\n□ Dry run\n□ Safe test data\n□ Limited pilot\n□ Customer review\n□ Acceptance test\n□ Staff training\n□ Production enablement\n□ First-week monitoring\n□ 30-day review scheduled\n\nOFFBOARDING READINESS\n□ Credential revocation list\n□ Data export/handoff plan\n□ Client-owned documentation\n□ Retention/deletion policy\n□ Automation shutdown procedure\n''',
'Security Human Approval and Data Policy':'''SECURITY, HUMAN APPROVAL, AND DATA POLICY\n\nPRINCIPLES\nLeast privilege; client-owned access; separation between clients; explicit approval for high-impact actions; auditable activity; reversible operations where possible; secure secret handling; honest capability boundaries.\n\nALWAYS HUMAN-APPROVED UNLESS CONTRACTED CONTROLS SAY OTHERWISE\n• Sending sensitive or consequential external messages\n• Public publishing\n• Financial transfers or payment-account changes\n• Refunds, discounts, or binding commercial commitments\n• Deletion of business records\n• Legal, medical, employment, or safety-critical decisions\n• Credential or permission changes\n• Actions involving unusual customer disputes\n\nDATA HANDLING\nClassify public, internal, confidential, regulated, and prohibited data. Minimize collection. Do not place secrets in prompts, documents, logs, or repositories. Use dedicated identities and stable secret stores. Establish retention, backup, deletion, and incident procedures before production.\n\nAGENT SAFETY\nDefine allowlisted tools and domains, escalation behavior, maximum action scope, duplicate prevention, budget limits, failure alerts, and human override. Test prompt injection and malicious document content. Treat external content as untrusted data.\n\nCLIENT SEPARATION\nEach client receives isolated configuration, credentials, memory, files, logs, and delivery channels. Never reuse one client’s confidential content to train or serve another client.\n\nINCIDENT RESPONSE\nStop affected automation; preserve relevant logs; revoke or rotate exposed credentials; notify the designated contact; assess affected records; document cause and remediation; obtain approval before re-enabling.\n\nDISCLAIMER\nThis operational policy requires legal, privacy, insurance, and industry-specific review before serving regulated or high-risk customers.\n''',
'Implementation and Acceptance Playbook':'''IMPLEMENTATION AND ACCEPTANCE PLAYBOOK\n\nPHASE 1 — CONFIRM THE OUTCOME\nWrite one measurable business outcome and one workflow owner. Reject vague “use AI everywhere” projects.\n\nPHASE 2 — MAP THE PROCESS\nDocument trigger, data, decision points, exceptions, approvals, outputs, systems, timing, and failure consequences.\n\nPHASE 3 — DESIGN CONTROLS\nUse least privilege, dedicated identities, secret isolation, approval gates, spend limits, logs, alerts, and a shutdown path.\n\nPHASE 4 — BUILD\nInstall and verify Hermes; configure provider and fallbacks; enable only needed tools; create client-specific memory and skills; connect approved systems; implement the narrow workflow.\n\nPHASE 5 — TEST\nTest normal cases, edge cases, missing data, duplicates, malicious content, API failures, expired credentials, rate limits, and human rejection. Never substitute fabricated success for a failed integration.\n\nPHASE 6 — PILOT\nUse a controlled audience or draft-only mode. Compare output with the prior manual process. Log failures and corrections.\n\nPHASE 7 — ACCEPTANCE\nCustomer confirms the workflow meets written metrics and controls. Deliver architecture, credentials inventory without secrets, SOP, shutdown instructions, known limitations, and support route.\n\nPHASE 8 — MANAGED OPERATION\nMonitor failures and costs; review quality; update skills; repair integrations; report value; propose the next capability only after the current one is stable.\n''',
'Monthly Client Performance Report Template':'''MONTHLY CLIENT PERFORMANCE REPORT\n\nCLIENT: [Name]   PERIOD: [Month]\n\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY\nWhat the AI operator completed, what improved, what requires attention, and the single best next action.\n\nBUSINESS OUTCOMES\n• Hours of manual work avoided\n• Estimated labor value saved\n• Leads processed and requiring follow-up\n• Median response-time change\n• Revenue opportunities influenced\n• Errors or missed items prevented\n• Customer-service volume supported\n\nSYSTEM HEALTH\n• Successful runs\n• Failed runs\n• Human approvals requested/accepted/rejected\n• Integration or credential incidents\n• Model/API usage cost\n• Availability and response quality observations\n\nIMPROVEMENTS COMPLETED\nList each approved workflow, prompt, skill, integration, or reporting improvement and its effect.\n\nRISKS AND OPEN ITEMS\nList exceptions, data-quality issues, scope questions, security concerns, and owner decisions needed.\n\nNEXT MONTH\nOne stability improvement; one business optimization; one proposed expansion with expected value.\n''',
'Case Study and Proof Template':'''CASE STUDY AND PROOF TEMPLATE\n\nCLIENT PROFILE\nIndustry, size, service area, systems, and anonymization requirements.\n\nBEFORE\nDescribe the workflow, delays, manual effort, missed revenue, errors, and owner burden. Include a measurable baseline.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION\nDescribe the narrow outcome, connected systems, human approvals, deployment timeline, and safeguards. Avoid exposing secrets or proprietary client data.\n\nAFTER\nReport measured changes in response time, hours saved, volume processed, error rate, opportunities recovered, revenue influenced, and software/operating cost. Separate measured facts from estimates.\n\nCUSTOMER QUOTE\nObtain written approval for the exact quote, name, logo, and publication channels.\n\nLESSONS\nWhat worked, what failed, what changed, and what future customers should expect.\n\nONE-SENTENCE PROOF\n“We helped [type of business] improve [metric] from [baseline] to [result] by implementing [workflow] with [control].”\n''',
'Landing Page and Outreach Copy':'''LANDING PAGE AND OUTREACH COPY\n\nHERO\nInstall an AI operator that actually helps run your business.\nWe connect a managed agentic AI system to the tools you already use so it can complete approved work, organize opportunities, and improve with your business.\nCTA: Book an AI Opportunity Audit\n\nWHY NOW\nSmall businesses do not need another chatbot or pile of disconnected subscriptions. They need reliable workflows that save owner time, respond faster, and protect valuable opportunities.\n\nWHAT IT CAN HELP WITH\nLead response; customer-request organization; follow-up; estimates and proposals; owner briefings; email and document operations; review requests; marketing drafts; research; recurring reporting; knowledge and SOPs.\n\nHOW IT WORKS\n1. Find the highest-value bottleneck.\n2. Install and connect a secure AI operator.\n3. Test it with human approvals.\n4. Launch one measurable workflow.\n5. Monitor, improve, and add capabilities over time.\n\nDIFFERENTIATORS\nOutcome-first; managed implementation; human control; client-separated data; documented systems; measurable reports; no requirement to replace every existing tool.\n\nSHORT OUTREACH\n“Hi [Name]—I help owner-operated businesses reduce lead leakage and repetitive admin by installing a managed AI operator across the tools they already use. I’m not selling a generic chatbot. We start with one measurable workflow, keep sensitive actions under human approval, and report the result. If lead response, follow-up, or owner overload is costing your business, I’d be glad to map the best first automation.”\n\nOBJECTION: CAN’T I INSTALL AI MYSELF?\n“Yes. The software is not the expensive part. Customers hire us to map the workflow, secure access, handle exceptions, test it against real operations, monitor failures, and keep improving it without making the owner become an AI infrastructure engineer.”\n''',
'Pricing Economics and Guardrails':'''PRICING ECONOMICS AND GUARDRAILS\n\nVALUE PRINCIPLE\nTarget approximately 15–30% of conservative first-year measurable value while preserving a clear customer payback. Do not price only by hours.\n\nBASELINE PRICING\nAudit: $750\nStarter: $3,500 setup + $1,250/month\nManaged Operator: $6,500 setup + $2,250/month\nGrowth Partner: from $12,000 setup + $4,500/month\n\nDIRECT COSTS\nClient should normally pay model/API, hosting, messaging, premium SaaS, and platform charges directly. If resold, include budget limits and margin for volatility.\n\nMARGIN TARGETS\nAim for strong gross margin after implementation stabilizes. Track sales time, discovery, build, testing, support, API cost, hosting, incidents, and monthly improvements. Increase price or narrow scope when support load threatens margin.\n\nCHANGE-ORDER TRIGGERS\nNew integration; new department; materially changed workflow; regulated data; custom app; migration; extra users; faster SLA; after-hours incident response; public publishing; complex data cleanup; additional monthly capability work.\n\nDISCOUNT RULES\nPrefer reduced pilot scope over discounted full scope. Exchange any founding-client discount for testimonial/case-study permission only after results, timely feedback, and a defined term. Never offer permanent unlimited support.\n\nGO/NO-GO\nReject clients lacking a workflow owner, legal authority to grant access, measurable outcome, willingness to test, or respect for security and human controls.\n'''
}

